{"hash":"be6e2a4f0199d431","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":113.453,"said":"You are in the sanctum of Shri Vishwanath, the Lord of the Universe. The walls around you are sheathed in gold. Look down: the Jyotirlinga is a short, smooth black stone set low in a square silver basin sunk into the floor, wet with water and milk, covered with bel leaves and flowers. This is the Lord who never leaves Kashi. Every evening the Saptarishi aarti is offered here by seven priests, one for each of the seven great sages, who, tradition says, come every evening to worship him themselves. And remember the vessel of Ganga water filled at the river’s edge? It will travel south to Rameshwaram, and when it is poured over Ramanathaswamy, by tradition, the Kashi yatra is complete. North and south, one pilgrimage. Fold your hands. Adi Shankaracharya sings of this place in the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Sanandam anandavane vasantam anandakandam hatapapavrindam, Varanasinatham anathanatham Shri Vishvanatham sharanam prapadye.’ ‘He who dwells in joy in the Forest of Bliss, the root of all bliss, destroyer of the heaps of sin, Lord of Varanasi, protector of those who have no protector: in Shri Vishwanath I take refuge.’ Kashi Vishwanath is the ninth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the damaru and the bells sound, and let the lamps be raised. Har Har Mahadev.\n\nDid you know? The day begins with the Mangala Aarti between 3 and 4 in the morning; devotees holding passes enter between 2:30 and 3.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1487},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1503,"end":1622}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,3],[0.314,0.192,4,7],[0.519,0.084,8,10],[0.615,0.084,11,14],[0.711,0.529,15,22],[1.252,0.156,23,25],[1.42,0.312,26,30],[1.745,0.685,31,41],[2.67,0.084,43,4